          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.093,
            0.078,
            0.182,
            0.096
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What if ."
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.058,
            0.113,
            0.203,
            0.221
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "atoms could combine in any ratio and not in a fixed ratio? How would this affect the substances around us?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.088,
            0.89,
            0.191
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The two laws discussed here formed the basis of "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Dalton’s Atomic Theory",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". This theory attempts to logically explain why substances combine in fixed proportions and why there is no loss or gain of mass during a chemical reaction. Dalton later explained these by proposing that during a chemical reaction, atoms are indivisible and merely rearrange, rather than being created or destroyed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.202,
            0.575,
            0.224
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9.3 Dalton’s Atomic Theory"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.227,
            0.889,
            0.312
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "John Dalton proposed his atomic theory in terms of certain postulates. These postulates combined earlier experimental observations and served as the basis for modern scientific theory. A postulate is a fundamental assumption accepted as truth without formal proof from which further ideas are formed or developed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.073,
            0.317,
            0.241,
            0.334
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Meet a Scientist"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.08,
            0.344,
            0.182,
            0.433
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.08,
                0.344,
                0.182,
                0.433
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_7.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.185,
            0.345,
            0.33,
            0.436
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "John Dalton",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " was born in England. In 1793, Dalton moved to Manchester to teach"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.07,
            0.437,
            0.331,
            0.546
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "mathematics, physics and chemistry at a college. He spent most of his life teaching and researching there. In 1808, he presented his atomic theory, which proved to be a turning point in the study of matter."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.363,
            0.316,
            0.619,
            0.333
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "John Dalton postulated that:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.372,
            0.336,
            0.888,
            0.37
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "All matter is made up of very tiny particles called atoms, which participate in chemical reactions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.372,
            0.369,
            0.888,
            0.402
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Atoms are indivisible particles, which cannot be created or destroyed in a chemical reaction."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.372,
            0.403,
            0.888,
            0.436
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Atoms of a given element are identical in mass and chemical properties."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.372,
            0.436,
            0.888,
            0.469
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Atoms of different elements have different masses and chemical properties."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.372,
            0.469,
            0.888,
            0.502
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Atoms combine in the ratio of simple whole numbers to form compounds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.372,
            0.502,
            0.888,
            0.535
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The relative number and kinds of atoms are constant in a given compound."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.364,
            0.535,
            0.888,
            0.57
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Dalton’s postulates provide the basis for the modern understanding of atoms and their behaviour."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.572,
            0.889,
            0.658
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "For example, hydrogen and oxygen atoms combine to form water, but the atoms themselves are not destroyed or changed into something else. Similarly, when magnesium burns in air, a white powder of magnesium oxide forms. This shows that the atoms of magnesium have combined with those of oxygen to form magnesium oxide."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.079,
            0.656,
            0.138,
            0.686
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 19,
              "bbox": [
                0.079,
                0.656,
                0.138,
                0.686
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_19.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.144,
            0.663,
            0.334,
            0.682
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pause and Ponder"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.089,
            0.692,
            0.853,
            0.741
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. Assertion (A): 2 g of hydrogen combines with 16 g of oxygen to form 18 g of water. Reason (R): According to Dalton’s Atomic Theory, atoms combine in a simple whole number ratio by mass to form compounds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.74,
            0.33,
            0.756
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Choose the correct option:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.756,
            0.624,
            0.773
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 24,
          "bbox": [
            0.107,
            0.773,
            0.65,
            0.79
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 25,
          "bbox": [
            0.104,
            0.79,
            0.327,
            0.806
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) A is true, but R is false."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 26,
          "bbox": [
            0.104,
            0.807,
            0.327,
            0.823
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iv) A is false, but R is true."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 27,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.851,
            0.855,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Later, scientists discovered how atoms combine to form "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "molecules",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Let us explore further!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 28,
